Scrolled layers do not move past their tiles.

The grey or white areas over part of the view while a heavy page is
scrolled (microsoft.com/de-de/windows: under the translucent sticky footer;
amazon.de product pages: up to the whole view) are the view background,
shown where the scrolling thread has moved the layers past the tiles the
main thread has made.  The scrolling tree positions layers on its own;
tiles are created and recorded only in a rendering update on the main
thread, so a main thread that is busy for longer than it takes to scroll
through the cover area leaves part of the view without tiles.

At every frame of a rendering update or of the scrolling thread the
compositor now checks, before it paints, whether every tiled layer has
painted tiles under each pixel it draws at the scrolling tree's positions.
If one has not, all layers are placed part of the way from the positions
the main thread last made tiles for towards the scrolling tree's ones:
the largest step, in whole device pixels, at which every layer is covered
(found by bisection).  Layers that are not covered even at the main
thread's positions (still loading, memory pressure) do not hold anything
back.  The scroll position itself is untouched; the layers catch up as
soon as the tiles arrive.

This trades the untiled area for the page standing still until the tiles
are there.  It is a stopgap: the cause is a main thread too slow for the
scroll, see tmp/PLAN.md.

